
 This is what C++ STL emplace uses. Allows you to reconstruct an object in place by reusing already allocated storage.

 This avoids constructing new objects which are then either copied or moved (overhead)

 While move is faster than copy, depending on implementation, it is not guaranteed to be zero overhead. Thus constructing an object
 in place is always considered SOL.

     // Declaration
     std::unique_ptr<float[]> data;

     // Memory allocation
     data = std::make_unique<float[]>(size);

     // Object allocation 
     new(&data[2]) float(payload);


 Above, we allocated the array upfront and then reconstructed an object directly in place using "Placement new"
 